  data:  # 3ba3
    kind: work_item  # 3ba3
    title: "py.py computes is_exported for module-level constructs but not for methods, so every Python method has no exportedness verdict"  # 3ba3
    status: todo_hard  # 3ba3
    priority: 3  # 3ba3
    parent:  # 3ba3
    tags:  # 3ba3
    - producer_gap  # 3ba3
    isbefore: []  # 3ba3
    duplicate_of: []  # 3ba3
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 3ba3
    pr_ref:  # 3ba3
    description: "INV-kubup made `Symbol.is_exported` a tri-state, so a record now says `null` where no producer computed exportedness. That turned a silent population into a visible one, and the largest single group is Python methods: on this repository's own survey, **23,466 of the 28,049 records that moved from `false` to `null` are `kind: method`**.\n\nThe cause is a producer gap, not a defect in the cure. `py.py` sets `is_exported` at four sites — module-level variables (`_is_python_top_level_exported`), classes, class attributes (`not attr.startswith(\"_\")`) and module-level functions — and at none for methods. So every Python method in every surveyed repository carries no exportedness verdict at all.\n\nWHAT A RULE WOULD HAVE TO DECIDE, stated rather than assumed:\n- A public method of an exported class is reachable from outside the package; a public method of a private class is not. So the honest rule is conjunctive, and needs the enclosing class's verdict — which `py.py` computes, in the same pass.\n- The leading-underscore convention applies to methods as it does to functions (`_helper` is private by PEP-8), and dunders are the public protocol, not private — `visibility.py::_is_python_private_name` already encodes exactly that and is the thing to reuse rather than re-derive.\n- `__all__` does not list methods, so membership is not the authority here; the class's own membership is.\n\nCONSUMERS THIS REACHES: the WI-zimum dead-code seed set in `exports` root mode, `cli`'s `exported_symbols` count, and the WI-zafab `--exclude-exported` filter. Today a Python method is invisible to all three, in both directions.\n\nNOT URGENT AND NOT A REGRESSION: before INV-kubup these records claimed `false`, which was worse (a phantom measurement). `null` is honest; this row is about supplying the measurement.\n\nREPRO: survey any Python repository and count `kind: method` nodes whose `supply_chain.is_exported` is null — it is all of them."  # 3ba3
    fields: {}  # 3ba3
- op: discuss  # 69bb
  at: '2026-09-22T01:28:55Z'  # 69bb
  by: agent  # 69bb
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 69bb
  clock: 2  # 69bb
  nonce: 69bb  # 69bb
  message: "PREMISE RE-MEASURED 2026-09-21. The mechanism holds; THE REPRO AS WRITTEN IS\nWRONG, and the correction makes this row SMALLER and better shaped.\n\nTHE ROW SAYS: \"count `kind: method` nodes whose supply_chain.is_exported is null\n-- IT IS ALL OF THEM\", and \"every Python method ... carries no exportedness\nverdict at all.\"\n\nMEASURED on this repository's own survey (.hypergumbo/self-analysis.json,\n47,148 python nodes):\n\n    python methods, is_exported = null    23,063   (97.2%)\n    python methods, is_exported = False      668   ( 2.8%)\n\nIt is not all of them. And the 668 are not noise -- the split is EXACT:\n\n    private-by-name (leading _, dunders excluded)  668  ALL is_exported=False\n    not private-by-name                         23,063  ALL is_exported=null\n\nZero exceptions in either direction.\n\nCONFIRMED ON A CONTROLLED FIXTURE through the full pipeline: a probe class with\na public method, a private method and __init__ yields is_exported=None for\npublic_method / add / workspace / __init__, and is_exported=False for\n_private_method. py.py itself emits None for EVERY method (verified by calling\nanalyze_python directly) and stamps modifiers=['private'] on the private one, so\nthe False is derived downstream from the modifier, not written by py.py.\nI did NOT chase which pass writes it -- stated as unknown rather than guessed.\n\nWHY THIS MATTERS FOR THE FIX. The row proposes a conjunctive rule and says the\nleading-underscore convention \"already encodes exactly that and is the thing to\nreuse rather than re-derive\". Half of that work is ALREADY IN EFFECT: the private\nhalf is decided, uniformly, today. What is missing is only the PUBLIC half -- and\nthat is exactly the half the row identifies as needing the enclosing class's\nverdict. So the deliverable is narrower than filed: supply a verdict for public\nmethods (conjunction with the class's own is_exported), leave the private path\nalone, and do not re-derive the underscore convention.\n\nTHE ASYMMETRY IS IN THE UNSAFE DIRECTION and is worth stating plainly: the\nmethods that DO have a verdict are the ones that could never be exported, and the\nmethods with NO verdict are the only ones that might be. For the WI-zimum\ndead-code seed set that means the seeds are unmeasured precisely where\nexportedness would change the answer.\n\nRoot cause as filed (\"py.py sets is_exported at four sites and none for methods\")\nis correct about py.py -- re-checked, the four sites are py.py:323 (module-level\nvariable), :3032 (class), :3086 (class attribute), :3350 (module-level function),\nand none covers a method. It is incomplete about the PIPELINE, per the above."  # 69bb
